          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "Your description did not match any emergency warning pattern in the tool I checked. That is not a clearance. If you develop severe or worsening symptoms, such as chest pain, severe headache, shortness of breath, confusion, or vision changes, seek urgent medical care.\n\nThe retrieved guidance does not say whether a home average of 150/95 needs action now. It says a diagnosis is not made from a single reading. It is based on an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. Home readings are one way to confirm that (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets). Your two weeks of readings are the kind of information a clinician would want to see, so it is reasonable to contact your doctor or clinic soon and share them, including how the readings were taken.\n\nFor the target, the same guidance says that for most treated adults, United States guidance describes a target below 130/80 mm Hg. It notes that the target is individualized for older adults with frailty, limited life expectancy, or many other conditions, and that the right target for you depends on your overall health (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets).\n\nThe retrieved material also mentions that regular aerobic activity, such as brisk walking, has a measurable effect on blood pressure (Physical activity for high blood pressure). Changes to diet, activity, or medication should be discussed with your clinician before you make them.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
